1
00:00:04,558 --> 00:00:14,748
Order a lab and file the result — Patients ▸ Patient History ▸ Lab Orders — Order the panel, track it, and file the result where it belongs — the chart.

2
00:00:23,651 --> 00:00:34,859
Lab orders belong on the chart. Order from here and the result comes back to the same record automatically.

3
00:00:34,859 --> 00:00:41,307
The Lab Orders tab is every panel ordered for this patient and where each one has got to.

4
00:00:41,307 --> 00:00:57,529
Status is the whole story — ordered, transmitted to the lab, then resulted and waiting for you.

5
00:00:57,529 --> 00:01:20,720
Attach the diagnosis. A panel with no clinical indication is a panel the payer will not cover.

6
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:26,392
Sent to the laboratory electronically, with an order number to track it by.

7
00:01:26,392 --> 00:01:32,663
When the result returns it lands on this tab as resulted, with abnormal values flagged.

8
00:01:32,663 --> 00:01:42,035
Sarah’s A1c came back at 8.2 percent against a range topping out at 5.6 — flagged, and worth a conversation.

9
00:01:42,035 --> 00:01:50,499
Review it, file it to the chart, and tell the patient. A result nobody acted on is the most expensive kind.

10
00:01:52,221 --> 00:02:06,561
What you just did

11
00:02:07,184 --> 00:02:16,184
Health | Efficiency | Growth — More in the Getting Started series — the next video is linked on screen.
